Salted Maple Chocolate Chunk Cookie
  --Sohla El-Waylly

2 c (240g) ap flour
3/4 tsp baking soda
1/2  baking powder
2/3 c (135g) packed dark brown sugar
1 tsp Fine Ground Sea Salt
1 large egg
1 tsp vanilla 
1 tsp maple extract
1 c (225g) unsalted butter
1/2 c (150g ) pure maple syrup
6 oz dark chocolate bars (~2 bars)
Light Grey Celtic Sea Salt

n the bowl of a stand mixer (or a large bowl if using a hand mixer), stir together the flour, baking soda, baking powder, brown sugar, and Fine Ground Sea Salt. Keep near the stove. In a small bowl, whisk together the egg and extracts. In a medium pot, combine the butter and maple. Set over medium-high heat and cook, occasionally stirring and scraping the sides of the pot. The mixture will foam and swell in the pot. Keep cooking until the foaming subsides and the mixture is deeply browned and the color of dark walnut wood, ~6-8 min. (The caramel may look broken; that’s okay.) Immediately and carefully pour into the center of the bowl of flour. Use the paddle attachment on medium speed to combine the maple and flour mixture until it comes together into a dough. Stream in the egg mixture, then increase the mixer to medium-high and beat until it no longer looks broken and greasy and becomes smooth and sticky, ~2 min. (Don’t worry about overmixing.) Use a #20 cookie scoop to portion the dough onto a sheet tray lined with parchment (3 tbsp or 60 g each). Wrap and chill in the fridge at least 12 and up to 48 hr. (You can then freeze the cookie balls and bake from frozen as desired. Add 1 min to the total bake time.) Set the oven racks into the upper and lower third of the oven. Line two sheet trays with parchment. Heat the oven to 375F. While the oven warms, chop the chocolate into 12 jagged pieces for pressing into the cookies straight out of the oven. Evenly space 6 cookies on each prepared sheet tray and bake, rotating the trays halfway through, until deeply browned along the edges, puffed, and set, ~12 min. Immediately press a piece of chocolate into the center of each cookie and sprinkle with Light Grey Celtic Sea Salt. Cool on the tray. Best eaten while warm.
